Step 16.2 – Connect the frontend to API Gateway
Purpose: Replace the placeholder in index.html with the actual HTTPS endpoint created in Step 15.4.

Run this command in your existing terminal:
sed -i '' "s|REPLACE_WITH_API_ENDPOINT|${API_ENDPOINT}/chat|" index.html


Verify the endpoint has been inserted:
grep 'const API_URL' index.html

=============
Step 16.3 – Create the frontend S3 bucket
Purpose: We will host the chatbot's static HTML frontend in Amazon S3. CloudFront will later provide HTTPS access to the application.

First, create a globally unique bucket name and create the bucket in Mumbai:
export FRONTEND_BUCKET="day2-rag-frontend-${ACCOUNT_ID}-ap-south-1"

aws s3api create-bucket \
  --bucket "$FRONTEND_BUCKET" \
  --region "$AWS_REGION" \
  --create-bucket-configuration LocationConstraint="$AWS_REGION"


============
Step 16.4 – Secure the frontend S3 bucket
Purpose: We will ensure the frontend bucket cannot be accessed publicly. CloudFront will access it through Origin Access Control (OAC), which we will configure later.

Run:
aws s3api put-public-access-block \
  --bucket "$FRONTEND_BUCKET" \
  --public-access-block-configuration \
  BlockPublicAcls=true,IgnorePublicAcls=true,BlockPublicPolicy=true,RestrictPublicBuckets=true \
  --region "$AWS_REGION"

=========
Step 16.5 – Upload the chatbot frontend to S3
Purpose: We will upload index.html to the frontend bucket. This makes the application available as an S3 object for CloudFront to serve.

Run:
aws s3 cp index.html "s3://${FRONTEND_BUCKET}/index.html" \
  --content-type "text/html" \
  --region "$AWS_REGION"

=============
Step 16.6 – Enable S3 Static Website Hosting
Run:
aws s3 website "s3://${FRONTEND_BUCKET}/" \
  --index-document index.html \
  --region "$AWS_REGION"

------------------

Step 16.7 – Allow public access to the static website
Purpose: Since we are serving the website directly from S3, browsers must be able to read index.html without AWS authentication.

We will allow public read access to objects in this frontend bucket only. Your documents bucket and vector bucket remain private.

Run:
aws s3api put-public-access-block \
  --bucket "$FRONTEND_BUCKET" \
  --public-access-block-configuration \
  BlockPublicAcls=true,IgnorePublicAcls=true,BlockPublicPolicy=false,RestrictPublicBuckets=false \
  --region "$AWS_REGION"


Now apply the bucket policy:
cat > frontend-bucket-policy.json <<EOF
{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "PublicReadForWebsite",
      "Effect": "Allow",
      "Principal": "*",
      "Action": "s3:GetObject",
      "Resource": "arn:aws:s3:::${FRONTEND_BUCKET}/*"
    }
  ]
}
EOF

aws s3api put-bucket-policy \
  --bucket "$FRONTEND_BUCKET" \
  --policy file://frontend-bucket-policy.json \
  --region "$AWS_REGION"

======

Step 16.8 – Open the chatbot website
Purpose: We will obtain the S3 website endpoint and verify that the frontend is accessible through a browser.

Run:
export WEBSITE_URL="http://${FRONTEND_BUCKET}.s3-website.${AWS_REGION}.amazonaws.com"

echo "$WEBSITE_URL"























































